A var handler written in a component that gets inlined per instance, whose
body writes a var of that same component.

`passNoInlineComponents` renames a callee's state per call site -- `count`
becomes `count__inst0` -- and applies the rename to the hoisted var inits, the
hoisted funcs and the timer handlers. Var handler bodies were not on that list,
so the spliced body kept writing the pre-inlining name: an assignment to a cell
the instance does not own, which every backend emits without complaint because
the name it writes is a declared one. Two `count`s in the golden below, and the
setter has to name the instance's.

The outer `count` is here to make that wrong write visible. Without a
same-named var in the enclosing scope the stale name resolves to nothing and
the emitters at least produce something undefined; with one it silently hits
the wrong variable.

deny out/none/html/index.html `state\.count = ` -- the setter writes the instance's own cell, not the outer one it was inlined into
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `m\.count = \(v \+ 1\)` -- same claim in Go: the receiver field has the instance suffix

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

component inner() {
    var count = 0

    var tracked = 0 @change(nv int) {
        count = nv + 1
    }

    vbox {
        button(text="+", @click {
            tracked = tracked + 1
        })
        text(value="{count}")
    }
}

component main {
    var count = 5

    vbox {
        text(value="{count}")
        inner()
    }
}
-- out/go/bubbletea/model.go --
